From: Alex Scotti <alexscotti@gmail.com>
Subject: clpi: the one-packet-early entry point lookup does not wrap at SPN 0

Found in review, traced in the code. libbluray-02 returns the packet
before an entry point from clpi_lookup_spn, so the entry point's own
packet is not cut off after a seek. For an entry point at SPN 0 that
was 0 - 1 = 0xFFFFFFFF: as a playitem's start_pkt, chapter 1's clip_pkt
or a time-seek target it lies past the end of the clip, every read
returns nothing, and playback stops at that seek. Real clips normally
open with PAT/PMT before their first entry point, which is why it is
rare. At SPN 0 the lookup now returns 0.
---
 src/libbluray/bdnav/clpi_parse.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/libbluray/bdnav/clpi_parse.c b/src/libbluray/bdnav/clpi_parse.c
index 2e989fd..f253a26 100644
--- a/src/libbluray/bdnav/clpi_parse.c
+++ b/src/libbluray/bdnav/clpi_parse.c
@@ -552,7 +552,13 @@ done:
     if (jj < 0) {
         jj = 0;
     }
-    spn = (entry->coarse[ii].spn_ep & ~0x1FFFF) + entry->fine[jj].spn_ep - 1;
+    spn = (entry->coarse[ii].spn_ep & ~0x1FFFF) + entry->fine[jj].spn_ep;
+    /* one packet early, so the entry point's own packet is not cut off
+     * (libbluray-02) - but an entry point at SPN 0 has no packet before it,
+     * and 0 - 1 wrapped to 0xFFFFFFFF: a start, chapter or seek target past
+     * the end of the clip */
+    if (spn > 0)
+        spn--;
     return spn;
 }
 
